{%- comment -%}
  One row of the bag — the boards' `Cart Line Item — Compact` (`ZUIpS`) and
  `Cart Line Item — Mobile` (`sH865`), used at every width.

  ONE ARRANGEMENT, NOT THREE. The 1440 board `UUnfX` draws this row as three text
  columns with the image between them; it is not what ships. See the note at the
  top of bf-cart-line.css for why. What matters here is that the markup below is
  the same at every width, and that only two things in it are not in the order
  they are drawn:

    - price sits next to the title in the DOM because that is the order the two
      are read out in; `order: 1` moves it under the options where it is drawn.
    - Delete comes before the stepper for the same reason — removing a line is
      the more consequential control, so it is reached first by keyboard.

  Both are one line each in bf-cart-line.css.

  Accepts:
    item         - a line_item from cart.items. Required.
    index        - 0-based position in cart.items. Required: the +1 of it is the
                   LINE NUMBER /cart/change.js addresses, and it is what
                   bf-main-cart.js reads back off data-line.
    remove_label - visible text of the delete control, e.g. 'Delete'.
    type_label   - prefix on the product-type line, e.g. 'Type:'.

  Usage:
    {%- for item in cart.items -%}
      {%- render 'bf-cart-line',
        item: item,
        index: forloop.index0,
        remove_label: section.settings.remove_label,
        type_label: section.settings.type_label
      -%}
    {%- endfor -%}

  The two visible strings are parameters because `render` isolates scope — a
  snippet cannot see `section`. Accessible names are NOT parameters: they come
  from Dawn's locale files, which are already translated into the thirty
  languages the theme ships, and a section setting would be one English string
  on every storefront.

  The line NUMBER, not item.key, is what goes on the wire. Both identify the
  line to Shopify, but a line number is only stable for the length of one render
  while a key is stable across renders — and since every mutation re-renders
  this whole section, the shorter-lived identifier is the one that cannot go
  stale behind us.

  PRICE IS THE LINE TOTAL, not the unit price. The board reads ₹2,198 against a
  quantity of 2 on a ₹1,099 tee, which is `final_line_price`.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign line = index | plus: 1
  assign discounted = false
  if item.original_line_price > item.final_line_price
    assign discounted = true
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  cart-line--first is what zeroes the row's 48px top padding. It is a modifier
  and not :first-child in the stylesheet because each row is wrapped in its own
  <bf-reveal> for the entrance, which makes every row the first child of
  something.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div class="cart-line{% if index == 0 %} cart-line--first{% endif %}" data-line="{{ line }}">
  <div class="cart-line__inner">
    <div class="cart-line__info">
      <a class="cart-line__title" href="{{ item.url }}">{{ item.product.title | escape }}</a>

      <div class="cart-line__price">
        {%- if discounted -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            <s> rather than a class, so the saving survives a stylesheet that
            failed to load — and the two visually-hidden labels because "₹2,198
            ₹1,999" read out in sequence says nothing about which is which.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'products.product.price.regular_price' | t }}</span>
          <s class="cart-line__price-was">{{ item.original_line_price | money }}</s>
          <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'products.product.price.sale_price' | t }}</span>
        {%- endif -%}

        <span class="cart-line__price-now">{{ item.final_line_price | money }}</span>
      </div>

      <div class="cart-line__variant">
        {%- comment -%}
          The board's two lines are `Type: Oversized T-Shirt` and `Size: L`. The
          first is product.type, the second a variant option, and they are drawn
          differently on purpose — the type is set back at 0.6 while the option
          carries the weight, because the option is the thing a customer checks
          before paying.

          Options are looped rather than pinned to "Size". Every product on the
          store has exactly one option today, but a tee that gained a colour
          would otherwise silently drop it from the bag.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if item.product.type != blank -%}
          <span class="cart-line__type">
            {{- type_label }}
            {{ item.product.type -}}
          </span>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- unless item.product.has_only_default_variant -%}
          {%- for option in item.options_with_values -%}
            <span class="cart-line__option">{{ option.name }}: {{ option.value }}</span>
          {%- endfor -%}
        {%- endunless -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          A property whose name starts with an underscore is Shopify's own
          convention for "internal, do not display" — apps stash state there.
          Blank values are skipped for the same reason Dawn skips them: an empty
          property is one an app set and did not fill in.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- for property in item.properties -%}
          {%- assign first_character = property.first | slice: 0 -%}
          {%- unless property.last == blank or first_character == '_' -%}
            <span class="cart-line__option">{{ property.first }}: {{ property.last }}</span>
          {%- endunless -%}
        {%- endfor -%}

        {%- if item.selling_plan_allocation != null -%}
          <span class="cart-line__option">{{ item.selling_plan_allocation.selling_plan.name }}</span>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- for discount in item.line_level_discount_allocations -%}
          <span class="cart-line__discount">
            {{ discount.discount_application.title }} (−{{ discount.amount | money }})
          </span>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      aria-hidden with tabindex -1: the title above already links to the same
      product, and two adjacent links to one destination make a screen reader
      announce every product in the bag twice.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <a class="cart-line__media" href="{{ item.url }}" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">
      {%- if item.image -%}
        <img
          class="cart-line__image"
          src="{{ item.image | image_url: width: 600 }}"
          srcset="
            {{ item.image | image_url: width: 300 }} 300w,
            {{ item.image | image_url: width: 600 }} 600w,
            {{ item.image | image_url: width: 900 }} 900w
          "
          sizes="(min-width: 1280px) 18vw, 36vw"
          alt=""
          width="{{ item.image.width }}"
          height="{{ item.image.height }}"
          loading="lazy"
        >
      {%- endif -%}
    </a>

    <div class="cart-line__actions">
      {%- comment -%}
        A LINK, not a button, and Dawn's own remove control is one too:
        `item.url_to_remove` is a real /cart/change URL, so deleting a line still
        works with the script absent. bf-main-cart.js intercepts the click and
        never lets the navigation happen when it is present.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <a
        class="cart-line__remove"
        href="{{ item.url_to_remove }}"
        aria-label="{{ 'sections.cart.remove_title' | t: title: item.title }}"
        data-remove
      >
        {{- remove_label -}}
      </a>

      {%- comment -%}
        <bf-quantity> is the PDP's stepper, reused whole: it owns stepUp/stepDown
        against the input's own min and max and disables the button that would do
        nothing. All it emits is a `change` on the input, which is the same event
        a customer typing a number produces — so bf-main-cart.js listens for one
        thing and never has to know which of the three raised it.

        min is 0, not 1: stepping down off the last one is how the boards' only
        other removal gesture works, and the stepper would otherwise stop at 1
        with the customer pressing a dead button.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <bf-quantity class="cart-line__qty">
        <button
          class="cart-line__step"
          type="button"
          data-step="down"
          aria-label="{{ 'products.product.quantity.decrease' | t: product: item.title }}"
        >
          {%- render 'bf-icon', icon: 'minus', size: '1em' -%}
        </button>

        <label class="visually-hidden" for="bf-cart-qty-{{ line }}">
          {{- 'products.product.quantity.input_label' | t: product: item.title -}}
        </label>
        <input
          class="cart-line__count"
          id="bf-cart-qty-{{ line }}"
          type="number"
          name="updates[]"
          value="{{ item.quantity }}"
          min="0"
          step="1"
          inputmode="numeric"
          autocomplete="off"
          data-quantity
        >

        <button
          class="cart-line__step"
          type="button"
          data-step="up"
          aria-label="{{ 'products.product.quantity.increase' | t: product: item.title }}"
        >
          {%- render 'bf-icon', icon: 'plus', size: '1em' -%}
        </button>
      </bf-quantity>
    </div>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    Per line, not per page: "you can only add 2 of this item" belongs beside the
    line it is about, and a customer who has scrolled to the fourth tee will not
    see a message parked at the top of the bag. Written by bf-main-cart.js from
    the quantity /cart/change.js actually granted.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <p class="cart-line__error" data-line-error role="status" hidden></p>

  <hr class="cart-line__rule">
</div>
